# Cat Mine Idle — environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values. `.env.local` is git-ignored and
# must never be committed. This file holds placeholders only.
#
# The split below is a security boundary, not a naming convention:
#
#   VITE_*  Vite inlines these into the browser bundle. Everything here is
#           public the moment the game is served. Only publishable values.
#   others  Read by the Supabase CLI and by Edge Functions. These never reach
#           the client bundle, and `npm run scan:secrets` fails the build if one
#           does.
#
# Local values for a running stack come from `npx supabase status`. They are
# fixed, well-known development keys — real ones only ever live in the deployed
# project's configuration.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ public --
# Supabase API URL. Local default: http://127.0.0.1:54321
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# Supabase anon/publishable key. Safe in the browser: it carries no privileges
# beyond what row-level security grants the caller.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ------------------------------------------------------------ server only --
# Service-role key. Bypasses row-level security completely and is the only
# writer of the `saves` table (Step 15). It must never appear in the client
# bundle, in this repository, in a log, or in any client-reachable artefact.
# Never give this variable a VITE_ prefix.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# ------------------------------------------------- supabase CLI only (.env) --
# `supabase/config.toml`'s `env(...)` substitution (Step 10's
# `[auth.external.google]` block) is read by the Supabase CLI itself, and the
# CLI only auto-loads a file literally named `.env` at the project root — not
# `.env.local`. `supabase start`/`stop`/`reset` have no flag to point it
# elsewhere. So the two variables below go in a **second, separate git-ignored
# file, `.env`** (also covered by `.gitignore`), not in `.env.local` with
# everything above. Everything above this section still belongs in
# `.env.local` as usual.
#
# To create them: Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials →
# Create Credentials → OAuth client ID → Application type "Web application".
#   Authorized JavaScript origins: http://127.0.0.1:5173, http://localhost:5173
#   Authorized redirect URIs:      http://127.0.0.1:54321/auth/v1/callback
# (the fixed local GoTrue callback — no domain needed for local development).
# Restart the stack (`supabase stop && supabase start`) after changing these,
# the same way `enable_anonymous_sign_ins` (Step 8) required a full restart
# rather than just `supabase db reset`.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-oauth-client-id
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-oauth-client-secret

# ------------------------------------ edge function runtime only (a THIRD, --
# ------------------------------- separate file: supabase/functions/.env) --
# For a test-only stack (a fresh clone, or CI) the three values below can be
# generated with `node scripts/write-functions-env.mjs`: it takes the two that
# must match from the fixture constants the tests already commit, generates a
# pepper, and refuses to overwrite an existing file. Use real values here only
# when you actually need a real bot.
#
# Server-milestone Step 12: `supabase/functions/telegram-sign-in/index.ts`
# reads `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` via `Deno.env.get(...)` at request time. Local
# Edge Functions get their runtime environment from a file at
# **`supabase/functions/.env`** — auto-loaded by `supabase start` — which is
# neither the root `.env` above (read only by the Supabase CLI's own
# `env(...)` config.toml substitution) nor `.env.local` (read only by
# Vite/Node). Three separate git-ignored files, three separate readers; see
# `README.md`'s "Local server stack" section for the full breakdown.
# Restart the stack after changing this file, the same as the other two.
#
# To create a bot token: message @BotFather on Telegram, `/newbot`, follow
# the prompts. Never expose this token to a client — it verifies `initData`
# server-side only (`memory-bank/server-threat-model.md` F1 records that a
# real Telegram Mini App host — the thing that would actually deliver a
# live, Telegram-signed `initData` to the game — is separate, later,
# optional work; this token is enough on its own to exercise the full
# verification/session-minting path with self-signed test vectors).
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=your-telegram-bot-token

# Server-milestone Step 14: `supabase/functions/recovery-code/index.ts` reads
# this pepper via `Deno.env.get(...)` to compute the HMAC-SHA-256 digest
# `recovery_codes.code_hash` stores — never the plaintext code, and never
# stored in the database itself, so a database leak alone does not permit
# offline enumeration. This was originally (Step 3) documented under the
# `.env.local` section above, before this step existed to establish where the
# value is actually *read*: like `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN`, it belongs here, not in
# `.env.local`, which the Edge Function runtime never sees.
#
# Generate one with: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
RECOVERY_CODE_PEPPER=generate-a-random-32-byte-value

# Server-milestone Step 14 review finding (2026-09-13): once
# `extractCallerAddress` reads the platform gateway's own trusted
# `X-Forwarded-For` hop rather than the client-suppliable one, every request
# a local integration-test run makes shares one real, gateway-observed
# rate-limit bucket — there is no client-side way to get a fresh one, by
# design. `tests/server-integration/recovery-code.integration.test.ts` resets
# that shared bucket between runs by calling
# `POST /v1/test-only-reset-rate-limit` with this token in an
# `X-Test-Reset-Token` header. Leave this UNSET in any real deployment —
# `checkTestResetAuthorizationViaEnv` refuses every request, including one
# carrying no token at all, whenever it is unset, so the route answers
# nothing at all until a value is deliberately configured. Generate one with
# `openssl rand -hex 32`, same as the pepper above; it is not a cryptographic
# secret protecting player data, only a local test-harness convenience, but
# there is no reason to make it guessable either.
RECOVERY_CODE_TEST_RESET_TOKEN=
